1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,946
Aarav pedals to college.

2
00:00:01,946 --> 00:00:04,406
His two legs push, one after the other.

3
00:00:04,756 --> 00:00:06,381
Left leg, then right leg.

4
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:08,587
The bike gets a push every half turn.

5
00:00:08,937 --> 00:00:11,334
Now imagine only one leg could push.

6
00:00:11,334 --> 00:00:13,381
The ride would jerk and crawl.

7
00:00:13,731 --> 00:00:16,840
A half-wave rectifier is
that one-legged rider.

8
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,650
Can we use both halves?

9
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:22,392
Remember the one-way flap
from the last episode.

10
00:00:22,742 --> 00:00:25,243
It throws away every backward push.

11
00:00:25,243 --> 00:00:27,099
Half the wave is wasted.

12
00:00:27,449 --> 00:00:31,514
The fix is to add more one-way
flaps, in a clever pattern.

13
00:00:31,864 --> 00:00:35,708
Then the backward push gets turned
around, instead of thrown away.

14
00:00:36,308 --> 00:00:40,413
Using both halves of the wave is
called full-wave rectification.

15
00:00:41,063 --> 00:00:45,654
The first way uses a special
transformer, with a wire from its middle.

16
00:00:46,004 --> 00:00:48,505
That middle wire is called a centre tap.

17
00:00:48,855 --> 00:00:52,756
It splits the coil into two
equal halves, top and bottom.

18
00:00:53,106 --> 00:00:56,162
Add two diodes, D one
and D two, and a load.

19
00:00:56,512 --> 00:00:57,989
Positive half cycle.

20
00:00:57,989 --> 00:01:00,913
D one is forward biased, so it conducts.

21
00:01:01,263 --> 00:01:03,348
D two is reverse biased.

22
00:01:03,348 --> 00:01:04,325
It rests.

23
00:01:04,675 --> 00:01:06,173
Negative half cycle.

24
00:01:06,173 --> 00:01:08,930
Now D two conducts, and D one rests.

25
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:10,304
Watch the load.

26
00:01:10,304 --> 00:01:13,074
Current goes through it
the same way both times.

27
00:01:13,424 --> 00:01:15,598
So every half cycle makes a hump.

28
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:16,610
No more gaps.

29
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:19,705
The second way needs no centre tap.

30
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:21,210
It uses four diodes.

31
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,374
They sit in a diamond, called a bridge.

32
00:01:24,724 --> 00:01:25,805
Positive half.

33
00:01:25,805 --> 00:01:29,399
D one and D three conduct,
in series with the load.

34
00:01:29,749 --> 00:01:30,805
Negative half.

35
00:01:30,805 --> 00:01:32,800
D two and D four take over.

36
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:36,283
Either way, current crosses
the load in the same direction.

37
00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:39,811
Two diodes work at a time,
while the other two rest.

38
00:01:40,461 --> 00:01:42,580
Full-wave means no half is wasted.

39
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:44,651
Every half cycle becomes a hump.

40
00:01:45,451 --> 00:01:47,097
So how much DC do we get now?

41
00:01:47,447 --> 00:01:49,211
Call the input peak V m.

42
00:01:49,211 --> 00:01:51,520
The humps now fill the whole time.

43
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,417
One hump averages two V m over pi.

44
00:01:55,117 --> 00:01:57,099
No gaps to share it with.

45
00:01:57,099 --> 00:01:59,497
So V d c is two V m over pi.

46
00:02:00,197 --> 00:02:03,357
That is about zero point
six three seven V m.

47
00:02:03,357 --> 00:02:05,182
Double the half-wave value.

48
00:02:05,882 --> 00:02:08,025
Current follows the same rule.

49
00:02:08,025 --> 00:02:10,366
I d c equals two I m over pi.

50
00:02:11,016 --> 00:02:13,533
Now compare the two circuits
on the numbers exams love.

51
00:02:13,883 --> 00:02:15,620
First, output frequency.

52
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:18,411
Two humps come out for every input cycle.

53
00:02:18,761 --> 00:02:22,654
So fifty hertz in gives one hundred
hertz out, for both circuits.

54
00:02:23,254 --> 00:02:25,192
Next, peak inverse voltage.

55
00:02:25,542 --> 00:02:29,242
In the centre-tap circuit, a
resting diode faces two V m.

56
00:02:29,592 --> 00:02:33,051
In the bridge, each resting
diode faces only V m.

57
00:02:33,651 --> 00:02:37,878
But the bridge current passes two
diodes, so it loses two diode drops.

58
00:02:38,228 --> 00:02:42,632
And the best efficiency doubles too,
to eighty one point two percent.

59
00:02:43,282 --> 00:02:44,363
Worked example.

60
00:02:44,363 --> 00:02:48,080
A bridge rectifier gets twenty
volts peak, at fifty hertz.

61
00:02:48,430 --> 00:02:50,123
The load is one kilo-ohm.

62
00:02:50,123 --> 00:02:51,613
The diodes are ideal.

63
00:02:51,963 --> 00:02:54,827
V d c equals two times twenty over pi.

64
00:02:54,827 --> 00:02:57,063
About twelve point seven volts.

65
00:02:57,663 --> 00:03:01,889
I d c is twelve point seven
volts over one thousand ohms.

66
00:03:02,489 --> 00:03:04,494
That is about twelve
point seven milliamperes.

67
00:03:05,094 --> 00:03:08,699
Each resting diode must
survive twenty volts backwards.

68
00:03:09,299 --> 00:03:12,114
And the humps arrive one
hundred times a second.

69
00:03:12,764 --> 00:03:14,225
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:14,575 --> 00:03:18,817
Many students say the centre-tap
diode faces only V m backwards.

71
00:03:19,167 --> 00:03:20,203
It does not.

72
00:03:20,203 --> 00:03:22,908
It faces the whole coil, so two V m.

73
00:03:23,558 --> 00:03:24,708
Where is this asked?

74
00:03:24,708 --> 00:03:26,452
PUC, CBSE and ISC board papers.

75
00:03:26,802 --> 00:03:31,265
NEET twenty twenty five asked which
diode conducts at a given instant.

76
00:03:31,615 --> 00:03:35,305
Expect to draw both circuits,
compare them, or find the DC value.

77
00:03:35,955 --> 00:03:36,855
Try this.

78
00:03:36,785 --> 00:03:40,743
Thirty sine one hundred pi t
volts feeds a bridge rectifier.

79
00:03:41,093 --> 00:03:42,876
The load is two kilo-ohms.

80
00:03:42,876 --> 00:03:45,876
Find the DC load current
and output frequency.

81
00:03:51,476 --> 00:03:52,376
C.

82
00:03:51,817 --> 00:03:54,879
Nine point five five milliamperes,
at one hundred hertz.

83
00:03:55,229 --> 00:03:57,289
The peak is thirty volts.

84
00:03:57,289 --> 00:03:59,613
So V d c is sixty over pi.

85
00:03:59,963 --> 00:04:02,384
That is about nineteen point one volts.

86
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:04,430
Divide by two thousand ohms.

87
00:04:04,430 --> 00:04:06,970
That gives nine point
five five milliamperes.

88
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,141
The input is fifty hertz.

89
00:04:09,141 --> 00:04:11,893
Full-wave doubles it to one hundred hertz.

90
00:04:12,543 --> 00:04:14,089
Screenshot this card.

91
00:04:17,289 --> 00:04:19,016
Next, the capacitor filter.

92
00:04:19,016 --> 00:04:21,677
How do we smooth these
humps into steady DC?
